Z||000000||theogre||04-27-2001||11:35 PM||||Sometime in the next few days there will be a global update of the cave. The site will be unavailable while that happens.<P>Of main interest will be the recall info page. I found that the Michigan Fiero Club site has most of the recall full text online. (Thankyou to them and Hopefully GM won't notice... That material is copyrighted....)<P>there is also a link to one that Michigan doesn't have...<P>I strongly recomend you all save/print these recalls while you can. This information is very important in a number of ways. For one thing it supercedes material in shop service books. ((Example. 84 motors should now use Torque To Yield bolts for ALL head remove/replace. The spec for this is in one of the recalls. It isn't in the service books or most parts books.))<P>The rest is mainly more cleanup to make it conform closer to W3C standards to cascading styles. This should make the pages display better on all browsers. (It won't change anything for IE 5+ users)<P>It will also introduce "terms of use" rules to the site. This is because some people can't seem to manage their own lives w/o being told how. It's mainly a clarification of existing warnings spread thru the site already. Also includes exactly how little logging I do. IE Privacy Policy.(The only thing I watch is total hits on files to see what people are reading. There are no cookies or anything else in my code.)<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-28-2001||10:57 PM||||do you mean that even after all the different warnings, and "If you screw up it aint my fault stupid a**" cautions, people still complain to you??<P>I just can't understand that!  I guess people just dont want to be held responsible for thier own mistakes.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-29-2001||11:45 PM||||That's about the size of it Joe... There's always some few who have to be a pain.<P><BR>The update is in progress now. I think it's near done.<P>Thanks to a picture posted in a forum thread I now have a replica of the Canadian recall label posted. I don't have Canadian recall codes or decent links. If any one up north has that data then I'll be happy to add it. (GM Canada doesn't use the same codes as U.S.)<P>Again, I recomend all reader read the recalls, even if they have already been done. <P>This is Especially important for you 84 owners. Your problems go beyond a batch of weak rods. 84's that have not been thru recall MUST NEVER be run low on oil. The recal clearly states that even 1/2 quart low can fry the 84 motor. Even after Recall the 84's must be watched. This includes 84's with dealer replaced motors which may still have the 84 oil pan on them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||04-30-2001||06:03 PM||||Was the oil pan smaller on the 84 Duke's compared to the later years?<P>Just wondering...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-30-2001||11:58 PM||||I've been told it was smaller pan, but I have no way to verify that. The new dipstick is calibrated for it to use 4 quarts with the larger filter.<P>The part that go my attention was the fact you can fry an 84 by running it even 1/2 quart low before the recal is done. That's pretty tight. How many cars on the road aren't at least a 1/2 quart short.... <P>Even after recall you figer the new filter holds another cup or so. You still need to make sure they are never allowed to get low.<P>The weak rods that have been claimed aren't the only reason the engine dies. Running them a little sort on oil can starve the crank and cook off the bearings.<P>One section of the 15B recall for 84 also indicates where to look for a split block.<P>To confuse things more.... never forget many 84's have already have new motors. So you have to verify just what motor went into them. I definatly would not take an original 84 motor and use it in a later car if I could avoid it.<P>Something I'd consider if I had to take the pan off is installing a dyke in the pan to keep oil in the sump during cornering. This is mentioned as part of the problem in the 84 recall. This <I>should</I> help reduce the starving problem. (I suspect the V6/V8 cars could also benifit from this fairly simple pan modification.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||1
